<p><b>The Law of Akela: Transformation of Methods of Interspecies Competition into Intraspecies Dynamics</b></p>

<p><b>Author:</b> Andriy Nosalsky<br>
<b>Publication Date:</b> September 11, 2026 (Revised edition of October 6, 2019)</p>

<hr>

<h3>Summary & Abstract</h3>
<p>This interdisciplinary monograph explores the foundational determinants of global political, economic, and civilizational divides by combining framework models from evolutionary biology, psychology, history, and international relations. The work argues that traditional geopolitical and macroeconomic indicators (such as GDP or geography) often confuse cause and effect by neglecting the socio-psychological and socio-biological mechanisms operating within individual and collective unconsciousness.</p>

<p>The author analyzes the evolutionary transition from matriarchal, partnership-based leadership models to aggressive, dominant patriarchal structures following the Neolithic Revolution and the emergence of agricultural surplus. Central to the narrative is the <i>"passionary factor"</i>—the impact of high-temperament individuals on state formation through mechanisms of natural and sexual selection.</p>

<p>Through key historical case studies—including the Fall of the Western Roman Empire, the Hunnic and Mongol invasions, Kievan Rus as a civilizational frontier, the rise of Marxism, and Soviet totalitarianism—the book illustrates how inter-generational trauma and codependent behavioral patterns shape modern political systems ("The Law of Akela / The Law of the Horde"). Finally, the work addresses contemporary threats, such as hybrid warfare, information manipulation, and social network algorithms used by anti-democratic regimes against Western democracies, emphasizing the civilizational role of frontier states like Ukraine in preserving human freedom and space-era technological progress.</p>

